      "title": "feedback 1 (brief)",
      "voiceover": {
        "text": "If the rotor matched the field's speed, there'd be no relative motion between them. The flux through the rotor's loops wouldn't change, so no emf would be induced, no current would flow, and there'd be no torque. The load and friction would slow the rotor until the slip, and the torque, returned. The classic slip is saying friction causes the lag. Friction only explains why the rotor needs a push. The lag is what makes the push, and at the field's speed there'd be no push at all."
      },
